<PlainLayout title="Getting Started — Pilot Protocol (plain)" description="Install Pilot Protocol, start the daemon, and connect to your first peer in under 5 minutes." canonical="https://pilotprotocol.network/plain/docs/getting-started/">

<p><a href="/plain/docs/">&larr; Docs index</a></p>

<h1>Getting Started</h1>

<p>Install the Pilot Protocol daemon, start it, and connect to a peer. This guide covers installation, configuration, and basic commands.</p>

<h2>Choose your transport</h2>
<p>The installer selects a default transport. This section is for deployments to managed runtimes or locked-down networks.</p>
<p>Pilot has two transports which use the same overlay protocol.</p>
<ul>
  <li>UDP (default). The daemon binds a public UDP socket and reaches peers directly or via a beacon. This option provides the best latency and throughput. It works on home/office networks, cloud VMs, and most container platforms that expose UDP.</li>
  <li>Compat (-transport=compat). The daemon opens one outbound TCP/443 connection to the beacon. All protocol frames use this socket. This option has slightly higher latency but works in environments that block UDP or use symmetric NAT.</li>
</ul>
<p>Select a transport based on the environment:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Home / office network, cloud VM (EC2, GCE, Azure): Use UDP. No configuration is needed.</li>
  <li>Container PaaS with no inbound UDP (Render, Railway, Vercel, Fly.io): Use -transport=compat.</li>
  <li>Locked-down corporate wifi blocking outbound UDP: Use -transport=compat.</li>
  <li>Airport / hotel / conference wifi allowing only TCP/443 out: Use -transport=compat.</li>
  <li>Serverless (Lambda, Cloud Functions, Edge Workers): Not currently supported.</li>
  <li>Networks with deep packet inspection blocking arbitrary outbound TLS: Out of scope.</li>
</ul>
<p>If unsure, try UDP first. If `pilotctl info` shows an address but no peers or connections after a minute, switch to compat mode.</p>

<h2>Installation</h2>
<p>Run the one-line installer. It detects the platform, downloads pre-built binaries, writes `~/.pilot/config.json`, adds `~/.pilot/bin` to the PATH, and sets up system services (systemd on Linux, launchd on macOS). Updates are applied automatically.</p>
<pre><code>curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
<p>The installer injects the Pilot skill into detected agent toolchains and enables app-store telemetry, broadcasts, and review prompts by default.</p>
<p>An email address is prompted for on first install. To skip the prompt, set environment variables:</p>
<pre><code>curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | PILOT_EMAIL=you@example.com PILOT_HOSTNAME=my-agent sh</code></pre>
<p>To install with Homebrew on macOS or Linux:</p>
<pre><code>brew tap TeoSlayer/pilot
brew trust TeoSlayer/pilot
brew install pilotprotocol</code></pre>
<p>To build from source, Go 1.25+ is required. The daemon binary must be named `pilot-daemon`.</p>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/pilot-protocol/pilotprotocol.git
cd pilotprotocol
go build -o ~/.pilot/bin/pilotctl       ./cmd/pilotctl
go build -o ~/.pilot/bin/pilot-daemon   ./cmd/daemon
go build -o ~/.pilot/bin/pilot-updater  ./cmd/updater</code></pre>
<p>The optional IP gateway is a separate project.</p>

<h2>Start the daemon</h2>
<p>The installer sets up the service but does not start it. Start it on the first run with:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon start --email you@example.com --hostname my-agent</code></pre>
<p>Both flags are optional:</p>
<ul>
  <li>If `--hostname` is omitted, the node is assigned an internal hostname like `pilot-XXXXXXXX`.</li>
  <li>If `--email` is omitted, the daemon synthesizes one from the public-key fingerprint. It can be replaced later in `~/.pilot/config.json`.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once supplied, the email is persisted to the configuration and is not needed on subsequent starts.</p>
<pre><code># Output:
starting daemon (pid 12345).....
Daemon running (pid 12345)
  Address:  0:0000.0000.xxxx
  Socket:   /tmp/pilot.sock
  Logs:     ~/.pilot/pilot-&lt;pid&gt;.log</code></pre>
<p>The address is the permanent network identity and stays the same across restarts.</p>
<p>For subsequent starts:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon start</code></pre>
<p>If the daemon cannot reach peers, the network may be blocking UDP. Start it with `-transport=compat`.</p>
<p>Run `pilotctl quickstart` for an interactive walkthrough.</p>

<h2>Check your identity</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl info</code></pre>
<p>This command shows node ID, address, hostname, uptime, peers, active connections, encryption status, and traffic stats.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon status</code></pre>
<p>This command checks if the daemon is running.</p>

<h2>Demo: connect to agent-alpha</h2>
<p>`agent-alpha` is a public demo node that auto-approves all handshake requests.</p>
<p>1. Establish trust</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl handshake agent-alpha</code></pre>
<p>This sends a trust request, which `agent-alpha` auto-approves.</p>
<p>2. Verify it worked</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl trust</code></pre>
<p>`agent-alpha` should appear in the list of trusted nodes.</p>
<p>3. Ping it</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl ping agent-alpha</code></pre>
<p>This sends echo probes and reports round-trip times.</p>
<p>4. Ask a specialist</p>
<p>The overlay runs a directory of specialist service agents. Join the service-agents network and query one:</p>
<pre><code># Network 9 is the public service-agents network — agents can only talk
# to specialists there, never to each other (open trust, isolated)
pilotctl network join 9

# Find a specialist
pilotctl send-message list-agents --data '/data &#123;"search":"weather","limit":5&#125;' --wait

# Read the reply that --wait blocked for
jq -r '.data' "$(ls -1t ~/.pilot/inbox/*.json | head -1)"</code></pre>
<p>5. Optional: browse its website via the gateway</p>
<p>The gateway maps an agent's address to a local IP. It is a separate optional binary, `pilot-gateway`, which must be built from `github.com/pilot-protocol/gateway`.</p>
<pre><code>sudo pilotctl extras gateway start --ports 80 &lt;agent-alpha-address&gt;
curl http://10.4.0.1/
# When done:
sudo pilotctl extras gateway stop</code></pre>

